import{_ as o,o as d,c,a4 as t}from"./chunks/framework.CGSRRTbQ.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Conformance with JVM Clojure","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/conformance.md","filePath":"guide/conformance.md"}'),a={name:"guide/conformance.md"};function r(s,e,n,i,l,h){return d(),c("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t(`<h1 id="conformance-with-jvm-clojure" tabindex="-1">Conformance with JVM Clojure <a class="header-anchor" href="#conformance-with-jvm-clojure" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Conformance with JVM Clojure&quot;">​</a></h1><p>cljam targets the semantics of JVM Clojure 1.12. This page records how that claim was tested, what matched, and what does not. The known divergences are generated from the project&#39;s knowledge base (<code>kb/entities/divergences/</code>), where each one is backed by a reference into the code.</p><h2 id="how-it-was-tested" tabindex="-1">How it was tested <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-it-was-tested" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How it was tested&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Three independent layers, each catching a different class of error.</p><h3 id="_1-clojure-semantic-suite" tabindex="-1">1. Clojure semantic suite <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-clojure-semantic-suite" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Clojure semantic suite&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>packages/cljam/src/core/__tests__/clojure_suite/</code> — 21 <code>.clj</code> files written in <code>clojure.test</code>, engine-independent, run through the session API. Several files mirror the <a href="https://github.com/jank-lang/jank" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">jank</a> test suite (<code>eq.cljc</code>, the <code>*_qmark.cljc</code> predicate files, namespace behaviour), adapted to the subset cljam supports. Areas: arithmetic, atoms, chars, collections, destructuring, edn, equality, error handling, hierarchies, higher-order fns, macros, math, metadata, multimethods, namespaces, predicates, sequences, sets, strings, transducers, vars.</p><h3 id="_2-differential-harness-walker-⇄-vm" tabindex="-1">2. Differential harness (walker ⇄ VM) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-differential-harness-walker-⇄-vm" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Differential harness (walker ⇄ VM)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>cljam has two execution engines sharing one analyzer front-end: the AST walker (the semantic reference) and a bytecode VM used for function bodies by default. <code>differential.spec.ts</code> evaluates every suite form on both engines and asserts identical results, including thrown values. 290/290 forms agree with 290/290 VM coverage. This guarantees the fast path cannot silently drift from the reference.</p><h3 id="_3-black-box-review-against-jvm-clojure-1-12-1" tabindex="-1">3. Black-box review against JVM Clojure 1.12.1 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-black-box-review-against-jvm-clojure-1-12-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Black-box review against JVM Clojure 1.12.1&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Six independent reviewers, given no knowledge of cljam internals or its known-gaps list, solved 16 practical challenges (collections, laziness, equality, destructuring, arity/recursion, macros, records/protocols, multimethods, state, dynamic vars, namespaces, reader, printing, exceptions, transducer pipelines, integration katas) on JVM Clojure and on cljam, then reported every observable difference with a minimal repro. Findings were deduplicated and ranked by how silently they fail.</p><p>Result: <strong>16/16 challenges scored PARTIAL</strong> — every area had a working core and at least one divergence. Nothing was unsolvable; nothing passed byte-for-byte end to end.</p><h2 id="what-matches-jvm-clojure" tabindex="-1">What matches JVM Clojure <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-matches-jvm-clojure" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What matches JVM Clojure&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Probed hard and came back value- or byte-identical, apart from the entries under <a href="#known-divergences">Known divergences</a>:</p><ul><li><strong>Destructuring</strong> — the full matrix: nested map/vector, <code>:keys</code>/<code>:strs</code>/<code>:syms</code>, namespaced and <code>::keys</code>, <code>:or</code> referencing earlier bindings, <code>:as</code>, <code>&amp; rest</code>, kwargs, nil sources.</li><li><strong>Functions and recursion</strong> — multi-arity/variadic dispatch, <code>recur</code> in fn and loop tails, both recur compile-error forms, <code>trampoline</code>, <code>letfn</code> mutual recursion, <code>partial</code>/<code>comp</code>/<code>juxt</code>/<code>apply</code>; 10M-iteration <code>loop</code>/<code>recur</code>; non-tail recursion to 100k frames (deeper than a default JVM stack) with a catchable overflow error.</li><li><strong>Macros</strong> — <code>defmacro</code>, syntax-quote/unquote/splicing into vector and set literals, auto-gensym, <code>macroexpand-1</code>/<code>macroexpand</code>, expansion-time helpers, recursive macros.</li><li><strong>Records</strong> — value equality, record ≠ map, assoc of an unknown key stays a record, dissoc of a basis key demotes to a map, <code>map-&gt;P</code>, positional <code>-&gt;P</code>, protocol dispatch after assoc.</li><li><strong>Protocols and multimethods</strong> — keyword and arbitrary-fn dispatch, <code>:default</code>, global <code>derive</code>/<code>isa?</code>/<code>parents</code>/<code>ancestors</code>/<code>descendants</code>, last-definition-wins, catchable missing-method and ambiguity errors.</li><li><strong>State</strong> — <code>swap!</code> (multi-arg, CAS), <code>swap-vals!</code>/<code>reset-vals!</code>, watches, volatiles, <code>delay</code>/<code>force</code>/<code>realized?</code>, <code>memoize</code>.</li><li><strong>Dynamic vars</strong> — <code>binding</code> nesting with restore on exit and on throw, <code>set!</code> inside <code>binding</code>, <code>with-redefs</code>, <code>alter-var-root</code>, forward declarations, and the lazy-seq binding-conveyance behaviour reproduced exactly.</li><li><strong>Exceptions on cljam&#39;s idiom</strong> — <code>ex-info</code>/<code>ex-data</code>/<code>ex-message</code>, <code>finally</code> on success and error, nested <code>finally</code> ordering, rethrow with data intact.</li><li><strong>Namespaces</strong> — multi-file projects via <code>cljam.sourceRoots</code>, transitive requires, <code>:as</code>, <code>:refer [syms]</code>, circular-require detection.</li><li><strong>Reader</strong> — <code>#_</code>, metadata forms, quote/var/deref/syntax-quote, char and string literals, dotted/slashed keywords, <code>::auto</code>, <code>#(… %1 %2 %&amp;)</code>, regex literals with named groups and inline flags.</li><li><strong>Printing</strong> — <code>pr-str</code> ↔ <code>read-string</code> byte-identical on nested structures; <code>*print-length*</code>/<code>*print-level*</code>.</li><li><strong>Laziness</strong> — creation is free, <code>lazy-seq</code> recursion, <code>take</code>/<code>drop</code>/<code>take-while</code>, <code>cycle</code>/<code>repeat</code>/<code>iterate</code>, <code>doall</code>/<code>dorun</code>, <code>mapcat</code>, <code>realized?</code>, <code>apply concat</code> spine realization.</li><li><strong>Transducers</strong> — <code>transduce</code>, <code>completing</code>, <code>into</code> with xf, <code>sequence</code> with xf, <code>partition-by</code>/<code>partition-all</code> as transducers, early termination, <code>dedupe</code>.</li><li><strong>Equality</strong> — cross-type sequential equality, equal ⇒ same hash including cross-type set/map lookup, stable sort, <code>min-key</code>/<code>max-key</code> ties.</li></ul><h2 id="known-divergences" tabindex="-1">Known divergences <a class="header-anchor" href="#known-divergences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Known divergences&quot;">​</a></h2><p>89 open and 3 justified differences. An open difference is a bug or an undecided question. A justified one carries its reason. Each was run on JVM Clojure 1.12 and on cljam.</p><h3 id="silent-wrong-value-no-error" tabindex="-1">Silent: wrong value, no error <a class="header-anchor" href="#silent-wrong-value-no-error" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Silent: wrong value, no error&quot;">​</a></h3><h4 id="pre-and-post-conditions-are-not-supported" tabindex="-1">:pre and :post conditions are not supported <a class="header-anchor" href="#pre-and-post-conditions-are-not-supported" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;:pre and :post conditions are not supported&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(defn f [x] {:pre [(pos? x)]} x)</code> then <code>(f -1)</code> throws <code>AssertionError</code> on the JVM and returns <code>-1</code> here, and <code>fn</code> behaves the same. The condition map is evaluated and discarded, so <code>(pos? x)</code> has no effect. <code>(defn f [x] {:post [(pos? %)]} x)</code> throws <code>Symbol % not found</code> here on every call, even when the JVM&#39;s condition holds.</p><h4 id="assoc-on-a-vector-skips-bounds-checks" tabindex="-1">assoc on a vector skips bounds checks <a class="header-anchor" href="#assoc-on-a-vector-skips-bounds-checks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;assoc on a vector skips bounds checks&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(assoc [1 2 3] -1 :x)</code> throws <code>IndexOutOfBoundsException</code> on the JVM. Here it prints as <code>[1 2 3]</code>, and the backing array holds a stray <code>&quot;-1&quot;</code> property. <code>(assoc [1 2 3] 1.5 :x)</code> throws <code>Key must be integer</code> there and prints <code>[1 2 3]</code> here. Past the end, <code>(assoc [1 2 3] 5 :x)</code> does throw.</p><h4 id="atom-options-are-ignored" tabindex="-1">atom options are ignored <a class="header-anchor" href="#atom-options-are-ignored" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;atom options are ignored&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(reset! (atom 0 :validator pos?) -1)</code> throws on the JVM and returns <code>-1</code> here. <code>(meta (atom 0 :meta {:x 1}))</code> is <code>{:x 1}</code> there and <code>nil</code> here. <code>set-validator!</code> works, but does not check the current value: <code>(set-validator! (atom 0) pos?)</code> throws on the JVM. <code>get-validator</code> and <code>reset-meta!</code> are absent.</p><h4 id="bit-operators-are-32-bit" tabindex="-1">Bit operators are 32-bit <a class="header-anchor" href="#bit-operators-are-32-bit" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Bit operators are 32-bit&quot;">​</a></h4><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>JVM</th><th>cljam</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>(bit-shift-left 1 40)</code></td><td><code>1099511627776</code></td><td><code>256</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(bit-shift-left 1 31)</code></td><td><code>2147483648</code></td><td><code>-2147483648</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(bit-and 4294967296 4294967296)</code></td><td><code>4294967296</code></td><td><code>0</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(bit-or 4294967296 1)</code></td><td><code>4294967297</code></td><td><code>1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(bit-not 4294967296)</code></td><td><code>-4294967297</code></td><td><code>-1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(unsigned-bit-shift-right -1 1)</code></td><td><code>9223372036854775807</code></td><td><code>2147483647</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The host&#39;s operators are 32-bit, but the number model does not bound them to 32, so this is wrong under any outcome of <em>One double-precision number type</em>. <code>bit-and-not</code>, <code>bit-test</code> and friends are in <em>Numeric tower API is absent</em>.</p><h4 id="case-accepts-duplicate-constants" tabindex="-1">case accepts duplicate constants <a class="header-anchor" href="#case-accepts-duplicate-constants" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;case accepts duplicate constants&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(case 1 1 :a 1 :b)</code> throws <code>Duplicate case test constant: 1</code> on the JVM and returns <code>:a</code> here. <code>(case :a :a 1 :a 2)</code> behaves the same. The list groups in <em>case rejects lists and symbols as constants</em> fail for a different reason.</p><h4 id="catch-does-not-test-classes" tabindex="-1">catch does not test classes <a class="header-anchor" href="#catch-does-not-test-classes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;catch does not test classes&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(try (throw (ex-info &quot;x&quot; {})) (catch Exception e :caught))</code> is <code>:caught</code> on the JVM. Here the exception escapes, and <code>(try (/ 1 0) (catch Throwable e :caught))</code> escapes too.</p><p>Core defines <code>Exception</code>, <code>Throwable</code> and a few other names as keyword stubs, and a keyword only matches a thrown map&#39;s <code>:type</code>. A name that does not resolve is a catch-all, so <code>(try (throw (ex-info &quot;x&quot; {})) (catch ArithmeticException e :caught))</code> returns <code>:caught</code> where the JVM rethrows. <code>(catch :default e ...)</code> catches everything. Open until the error and class model is redesigned; see <em>Thrown values are not exceptions</em>.</p><h4 id="class-type-and-instance-have-no-class-model" tabindex="-1">class, type and instance? have no class model <a class="header-anchor" href="#class-type-and-instance-have-no-class-model" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;class, type and instance? have no class model&quot;">​</a></h4><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>JVM</th><th>cljam</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>(class 1)</code></td><td><code>java.lang.Long</code></td><td><code>&quot;conjure.number&quot;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(class nil)</code></td><td><code>nil</code></td><td><code>&quot;conjure.nil&quot;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(type &quot;s&quot;)</code></td><td><code>java.lang.String</code></td><td><code>:string</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(type nil)</code></td><td><code>nil</code></td><td><code>:nil</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(type (with-meta [] {:type :x}))</code></td><td><code>:x</code></td><td><code>:vector</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(instance? String &quot;a&quot;)</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(isa? Long Number)</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>instance?</code> ignores its arguments and returns <code>false</code>, and the <code>conjure.</code> prefix is a leftover of an old project name. <code>defrecord</code> defines no class symbol: <code>P</code> does not resolve after <code>(defrecord P [x])</code>, where <code>(instance? P (-&gt;P 1))</code> is <code>true</code> on the JVM, and neither <code>(P. 1)</code> nor <code>(new P 1)</code> constructs one. <code>(defmulti f class)</code> with <code>String</code> as a dispatch value finds no method. <code>class?</code>, <code>parents</code> and <code>ancestors</code> on classes return <code>false</code> or <code>nil</code>. Open until the class model is redesigned together with the errors.</p><h4 id="clojure-core-carries-non-clojure-names" tabindex="-1">clojure.core carries non-Clojure names <a class="header-anchor" href="#clojure-core-carries-non-clojure-names" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;clojure.core carries non-Clojure names&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>make-record!</code>, <code>str-reverse*</code>, <code>hierarchy-isa?*</code> and <code>case-emit</code> resolve as <code>clojure.core</code> vars here, and so do cljam&#39;s own <code>describe</code>, <code>js-&gt;clj</code>, <code>clj-&gt;js</code>, <code>pprint</code>, <code>doc</code> and <code>then</code>. Each shadows nothing on the JVM, so a user definition with one of these names collides here.</p><p><code>Math</code>, <code>String</code> and the other class stubs evaluate to keywords. <code>(all-ns)</code> lists <code>clojure.reflect</code> and <code>cursive.repl.runtime</code>, stub namespaces for editor integrations.</p><h4 id="condp-has-no-form" tabindex="-1">condp has no :&gt;&gt; form <a class="header-anchor" href="#condp-has-no-form" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;condp has no :&gt;&gt; form&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(condp some [1] #{1} :&gt;&gt; inc)</code> is <code>2</code> on the JVM, which passes the predicate&#39;s result to <code>inc</code>. Here it is <code>:&gt;&gt;</code>, and the form after it is read as the next clause.</p><h4 id="delay-does-not-cache-a-thrown-exception" tabindex="-1">delay does not cache a thrown exception <a class="header-anchor" href="#delay-does-not-cache-a-thrown-exception" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;delay does not cache a thrown exception&quot;">​</a></h4><div class="language-clojure vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">clojure</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">let</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> [n (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">atom</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 0</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">      d (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">delay</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">swap!</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> n inc) (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">throw</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ex-info</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;x&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> {})))]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">  (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">try</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> @d (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">catch</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> :default</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> e </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">nil</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">  (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">try</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> @d (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">catch</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> :default</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> e </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">nil</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">  @n)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The JVM counts <code>1</code>, cljam counts <code>2</code>.</p><p>The oracle lists its cases under <em>catch does not test classes</em>, because <code>catch</code> with a class name fails first.</p><h4 id="duplicate-keys-in-literals-are-accepted" tabindex="-1">Duplicate keys in literals are accepted <a class="header-anchor" href="#duplicate-keys-in-literals-are-accepted" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Duplicate keys in literals are accepted&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>{:a 1 :a 2}</code> is a read error on the JVM (<code>Duplicate key: :a</code>) and <code>{:a 2}</code> here. <code>#{1 1}</code> is an error there and <code>#{1}</code> here, because <code>readSet</code> deduplicates.</p><h4 id="flatten-only-descends-into-lists-and-vectors" tabindex="-1">flatten only descends into lists and vectors <a class="header-anchor" href="#flatten-only-descends-into-lists-and-vectors" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;flatten only descends into lists and vectors&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(flatten (list (range 2)))</code> is <code>(0 1)</code> on the JVM and <code>[(0 1)]</code> here, and <code>(flatten (list (list 1) (seq [2])))</code> is <code>(1 2)</code> there and <code>[1 (2)]</code> here. <code>flatten-step</code> tests <code>list?</code> and <code>vector?</code>, where the JVM tests <code>sequential?</code>. It also returns a vector; see <em>Seq functions return vectors</em>.</p><h4 id="get-and-friends-miss-some-collection-types" tabindex="-1">get and friends miss some collection types <a class="header-anchor" href="#get-and-friends-miss-some-collection-types" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;get and friends miss some collection types&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(get #{1} 1)</code> is <code>1</code> on the JVM and <code>nil</code> here, though <code>(#{1} 1)</code> works. <code>(find [1] 0)</code> is <code>[0 1]</code> there and throws here, and <code>(contains? &quot;a&quot; 0)</code> is <code>true</code> there and throws here.</p><h4 id="hashes-and-hash-order-differ" tabindex="-1">Hashes and hash order differ <a class="header-anchor" href="#hashes-and-hash-order-differ" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Hashes and hash order differ&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(hash &quot;a&quot;)</code> is <code>1455541201</code> on the JVM and <code>-533258471</code> here; <code>(hash 1)</code> is <code>1392991556</code> and <code>1</code>. Equal values still hash equally.</p><p>Order follows from it. <code>(seq #{:a :b :c})</code> is <code>(:c :b :a)</code> on the JVM and <code>(:a :b :c)</code> here, and a map past eight entries such as <code>(zipmap (range 12) (range 12))</code> iterates in hash order there and insertion order here. Small map literals keep insertion order on both.</p><p>Absent vars: <code>hash-combine</code>, <code>hash-ordered-coll</code>, <code>hash-unordered-coll</code>, <code>mix-collection-hash</code>.</p><h4 id="identical-is-false-for-equal-keywords-and-numbers" tabindex="-1">identical? is false for equal keywords and numbers <a class="header-anchor" href="#identical-is-false-for-equal-keywords-and-numbers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;identical? is false for equal keywords and numbers&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(identical? :a :a)</code> and <code>(identical? :a (keyword &quot;a&quot;))</code> are <code>true</code> on the JVM and <code>false</code> here. So are <code>(identical? 1 1)</code> and <code>(identical? &quot;a&quot; &quot;a&quot;)</code>, which are <code>true</code> there through caching and interning. <code>=</code> works. Every keyword is a fresh box; <em>unboxed-values-transition</em> bears on this.</p><h4 id="iterate-calls-its-fn-too-early" tabindex="-1">iterate calls its fn too early <a class="header-anchor" href="#iterate-calls-its-fn-too-early" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;iterate calls its fn too early&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(let [n (atom 0)] (first (iterate (fn [x] (swap! n inc) x) 0)) @n)</code> is <code>0</code> on the JVM and <code>1</code> here. Taking two elements gives <code>1</code> and <code>2</code>. The fn runs even for the element nobody asks for, which matters when it has side effects or throws.</p><h4 id="leading-zeros-are-not-octal" tabindex="-1">Leading zeros are not octal <a class="header-anchor" href="#leading-zeros-are-not-octal" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Leading zeros are not octal&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>017</code> is <code>15</code> on the JVM and <code>17</code> here. <code>-017</code> is <code>-15</code> and <code>-17</code>. <code>08</code> is a read error on the JVM and <code>8</code> here.</p><h4 id="macro-expansions-have-a-different-shape" tabindex="-1">Macro expansions have a different shape <a class="header-anchor" href="#macro-expansions-have-a-different-shape" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Macro expansions have a different shape&quot;">​</a></h4><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>JVM</th><th>cljam</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>(macroexpand-1 &#39;(when a b))</code></td><td><code>(if a (do b))</code></td><td><code>(if a (do b) nil)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(macroexpand-1 &#39;(-&gt; a b))</code></td><td><code>(b a)</code></td><td><code>(clojure.core/-&gt; (b a))</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(macroexpand-1 &#39;(defn f [] 1))</code></td><td><code>(def f (clojure.core/fn ([] 1)))</code></td><td><code>(def f (clojure.core/fn f [] 1))</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&#39;#(+ %)</code></td><td><code>(fn* [p1__N#] (+ p1__N#))</code></td><td><code>(fn [p1] (+ p1))</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&#39;@a</code></td><td><code>(clojure.core/deref a)</code></td><td><code>(deref a)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&#39;~a</code></td><td><code>(clojure.core/unquote a)</code></td><td><code>(unquote a)</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Results of running the code are the same. Generated names differ too: <code>\`foo#</code> is <code>foo__35</code>, not <code>foo__391__auto__</code>. <code>(destructure &#39;[[a] b])</code> expands to a different binding vector.</p><h4 id="macros-win-over-local-bindings" tabindex="-1">Macros win over local bindings <a class="header-anchor" href="#macros-win-over-local-bindings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Macros win over local bindings&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(let [when (fn [&amp; _] :local)] (when false 1))</code> is <code>:local</code> on the JVM and <code>nil</code> here. <code>(let [and ...] (and 1 2))</code>, <code>letfn</code> and fn parameters behave the same way.</p><p><code>lookupMacro</code> looks the head symbol up without checking the locals in scope.</p><h4 id="map-destructuring-treats-vectors-as-kwargs" tabindex="-1">Map destructuring treats vectors as kwargs <a class="header-anchor" href="#map-destructuring-treats-vectors-as-kwargs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Map destructuring treats vectors as kwargs&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(let [{a 0} [10 20]] a)</code> is <code>10</code> on the JVM and <code>nil</code> here. <code>(let [{:keys [a]} [:a 1]] a)</code> is <code>nil</code> there and <code>1</code> here.</p><p>The JVM turns only seqs into a map. cljam applies <code>hash-map</code> to any sequential value, so <code>(let [{:keys [a]} [:a 1 :b]] a)</code> fails with a <code>hash-map</code> error.</p><h4 id="namespaces-have-no-metadata" tabindex="-1">Namespaces have no metadata <a class="header-anchor" href="#namespaces-have-no-metadata" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Namespaces have no metadata&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(meta (find-ns &#39;clojure.core))</code> is <code>{:doc &quot;Fundamental library of the Clojure language&quot;, :author &quot;Rich Hickey&quot;}</code> on the JVM and <code>nil</code> here. <code>(ns foo &quot;doc&quot;) (meta *ns*)</code> is <code>{:doc &quot;doc&quot;}</code> there, and <code>(ns ^:a foo) (meta *ns*)</code> is <code>{:a true}</code>. Both are <code>nil</code> here.</p><h4 id="one-double-precision-number-type" tabindex="-1">One double-precision number type <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-double-precision-number-type" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One double-precision number type&quot;">​</a></h4><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>JVM</th><th>cljam</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>(= 1 1.0)</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>true</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(count {1 :a 1.0 :b})</code></td><td><code>2</code></td><td><code>1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(str 1.0)</code>, <code>1e3</code></td><td><code>&quot;1.0&quot;</code>, <code>1000.0</code></td><td><code>&quot;1&quot;</code>, <code>1000</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(integer? 1.0)</code>, <code>(float? 1.0)</code></td><td><code>false</code>, <code>true</code></td><td><code>true</code>, <code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(/ 1 2)</code></td><td><code>1/2</code></td><td><code>0.5</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>9007199254740993</code></td><td>exact</td><td><code>9007199254740992</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(+ 9223372036854775807 1)</code></td><td>throws <code>long overflow</code></td><td><code>9223372036854776000</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(pr-str 1e21)</code></td><td><code>&quot;1.0E21&quot;</code></td><td><code>&quot;1e+21&quot;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(pr-str -0.0)</code></td><td><code>&quot;-0.0&quot;</code></td><td><code>&quot;0&quot;</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>int?</code> and <code>double?</code> answer <code>true</code> for every whole number, so <code>(double? 1)</code> is <code>true</code>. The box in <code>CljNumber</code> could carry a tag, so the host does not force this today.</p><p>Open pending a decision on the number model. The body records the reason once one is made.</p><h4 id="private-vars-are-not-enforced" tabindex="-1">Private vars are not enforced <a class="header-anchor" href="#private-vars-are-not-enforced" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Private vars are not enforced&quot;">​</a></h4><p>After <code>(ns a) (defn- hidden [] 2)</code>, calling <code>(a/hidden)</code> from another namespace is an error on the JVM (<code>var: #&#39;a/hidden is not public</code>) and returns <code>2</code> here. <code>:refer [hidden]</code> also succeeds. <code>(:private (meta #&#39;hidden))</code> is <code>true</code>, so the marker is recorded but unchecked.</p><h4 id="read-string-takes-no-options-and-accepts-empty-input" tabindex="-1">read-string takes no options and accepts empty input <a class="header-anchor" href="#read-string-takes-no-options-and-accepts-empty-input" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;read-string takes no options and accepts empty input&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(read-string &quot;&quot;)</code> is an <code>EOF while reading</code> error on the JVM and <code>nil</code> here. <code>(read-string {:eof :x} &quot;&quot;)</code> is <code>:x</code> there and throws <code>read-string expects a string</code> here. <code>^&quot;x&quot; []</code> is valid metadata on the JVM (<code>{:tag &quot;x&quot;}</code>) and a read error here.</p><h4 id="records-are-not-maps" tabindex="-1">Records are not maps <a class="header-anchor" href="#records-are-not-maps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Records are not maps&quot;">​</a></h4><p>With <code>(defrecord R [x])</code>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>JVM</th><th>cljam</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>(map? (-&gt;R 1))</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(associative? (-&gt;R 1))</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(meta (with-meta (-&gt;R 1) {:a 1}))</code></td><td><code>{:a 1}</code></td><td><code>with-meta does not support record</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(let [{:keys [x]} (-&gt;R 1)] x)</code></td><td><code>1</code></td><td><code>Cannot destructure</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>((fn [{:keys [x]}] x) (-&gt;R 1))</code></td><td><code>1</code></td><td><code>Cannot destructure</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The predicates answer wrongly with no error, so code that branches on <code>map?</code> takes the wrong path, and <code>destructure</code> is one such branch. <code>get</code>, <code>assoc</code>, <code>merge</code> and keyword lookup work on records.</p><h4 id="records-can-be-called-as-functions" tabindex="-1">Records can be called as functions <a class="header-anchor" href="#records-can-be-called-as-functions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Records can be called as functions&quot;">​</a></h4><p>With <code>(defrecord R [x y])</code>, <code>((-&gt;R 5 6) :x)</code> throws on the JVM and returns <code>5</code> here. <code>((-&gt;R 5 6) :w)</code> throws there and returns <code>nil</code> here. <code>(ifn? (-&gt;R 5 6))</code> is <code>false</code> and <code>true</code>.</p><h4 id="regexes-use-the-javascript-dialect" tabindex="-1">Regexes use the JavaScript dialect <a class="header-anchor" href="#regexes-use-the-javascript-dialect" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Regexes use the JavaScript dialect&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(re-find #&quot;\\Aa\\z&quot; &quot;a&quot;)</code>, <code>(re-find #&quot;\\p{Alpha}&quot; &quot;a&quot;)</code>, <code>(re-find #&quot;\\Qa.b\\E&quot; &quot;a.b&quot;)</code> and <code>(re-find #&quot;[a&amp;&amp;[^b]]&quot; &quot;a&quot;)</code> match on the JVM and return <code>nil</code> here. <code>(re-find #&quot;a++&quot; &quot;aa&quot;)</code> and <code>(?x)</code> are errors here.</p><p>The host engine is a fixed constraint: a regex is a host RegExp, as in ClojureScript, so a Java engine is not the goal. That does not cover the silent <code>nil</code>. <code>\\A</code>, <code>\\z</code> and <code>\\Q...\\E</code> can be translated to RegExp syntax, and syntax with no translation can be rejected at read time, as <code>(?x)</code> already is. Named groups, <code>(?i)</code>, <code>(?s)</code> and <code>(?m)</code> behave the same.</p><h4 id="seq-functions-return-vectors" tabindex="-1">Seq functions return vectors <a class="header-anchor" href="#seq-functions-return-vectors" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Seq functions return vectors&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(sort [2 1])</code> is <code>(1 2)</code> on the JVM and <code>[1 2]</code> here, <code>(seq? (sort [1]))</code> is <code>false</code>, <code>(vector? (sort [1]))</code> is <code>true</code>.</p><p>The same holds for <code>sort-by</code>, <code>keys</code>, <code>vals</code>, <code>flatten</code>, <code>rest</code> and <code>nthrest</code> on a vector, <code>cons</code> onto a vector, <code>distinct</code>, <code>drop-last</code>, <code>reductions</code>, <code>list*</code>, both parts of <code>split-at</code> and <code>split-with</code>, and <code>partition</code>, which returns a vector of vectors. <code>partition-all</code> and <code>partition-by</code> return a seq of vectors, so <code>(partition-by even? [1 1 2])</code> is <code>([1 1] [2])</code>.</p><p>Related differences in the same family: <code>(keys {})</code> and <code>(vals {})</code> are <code>[]</code> here and <code>nil</code> on the JVM, <code>(sort nil)</code> is <code>[]</code> where the JVM gives <code>()</code>, <code>(empty (map inc [1]))</code> is <code>nil</code>, not <code>()</code>, <code>(list? (rest &#39;(1 2)))</code> is <code>false</code> where the JVM says <code>true</code>, and <code>(list? (cons 1 &#39;(2)))</code> is <code>true</code> where the JVM says <code>false</code>.</p><h4 id="seqs-are-not-chunked" tabindex="-1">Seqs are not chunked <a class="header-anchor" href="#seqs-are-not-chunked" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Seqs are not chunked&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Values are identical, but side-effect counts differ. <code>(let [n (atom 0)] (first (map (fn [x] (swap! n inc) x) (range 100))) @n)</code> is <code>32</code> on the JVM and <code>1</code> here.</p><p>Absent vars: <code>chunk</code>, <code>chunk-append</code>, <code>chunk-buffer</code>, <code>chunk-cons</code>, <code>chunk-first</code>, <code>chunk-next</code>, <code>chunk-rest</code>, <code>chunked-seq?</code>.</p><h4 id="some-empty-seq-results-are-nil" tabindex="-1">Some empty seq results are nil <a class="header-anchor" href="#some-empty-seq-results-are-nil" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Some empty seq results are nil&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(concat)</code>, <code>(empty (map inc [1]))</code>, <code>(empty (range 3))</code>, <code>(empty (filter odd? [1]))</code> and <code>(empty (seq [1]))</code> are all <code>()</code> on the JVM and <code>nil</code> here. <code>(concat nil)</code> and <code>(concat [] [])</code> already return <code>()</code>. <code>(empty (rest [1]))</code> is <code>[]</code> here, as in <em>Seq functions return vectors</em>.</p><h4 id="some-invalid-arguments-return-a-value" tabindex="-1">Some invalid arguments return a value <a class="header-anchor" href="#some-invalid-arguments-return-a-value" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Some invalid arguments return a value&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(subs &quot;abc&quot; 5)</code> throws <code>StringIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> on the JVM and returns <code>&quot;&quot;</code> here. <code>(even? 1.5)</code> and <code>(odd? 1.5)</code> throw <code>Argument must be an integer</code> there and return <code>false</code> and <code>true</code> here. <code>(derive :a :b)</code> fails an assertion there, since the global hierarchy needs namespaced tags, and returns a hierarchy here. <code>(quote)</code> and <code>(quote 1 2)</code> throw <code>Wrong number of args</code> there and return <code>nil</code> and <code>1</code> here.</p><h4 id="some-lazy-functions-are-eager" tabindex="-1">Some lazy functions are eager <a class="header-anchor" href="#some-lazy-functions-are-eager" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Some lazy functions are eager&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(first (partition 3 (range)))</code> is <code>(0 1 2)</code> on the JVM and hangs here. So do <code>(first (distinct (range)))</code>, <code>(first (reductions + (range)))</code>, <code>(first (split-at 2 (range)))</code> and <code>(first (split-with #(&lt; % 3) (range)))</code>.</p><p><code>partition-all</code>, <code>partition-by</code>, <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>mapcat</code>, <code>take-nth</code>, <code>keep</code>, <code>interleave</code>, <code>interpose</code>, <code>dedupe</code>, <code>concat</code> and <code>drop-while</code> stay lazy.</p><h4 id="special-symbol-answers-from-cljam-s-own-list" tabindex="-1">special-symbol? answers from cljam&#39;s own list <a class="header-anchor" href="#special-symbol-answers-from-cljam-s-own-list" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;special-symbol? answers from cljam&#39;s own list&quot;">​</a></h4><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>JVM</th><th>cljam</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>(special-symbol? &#39;defmacro)</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>true</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(special-symbol? &#39;binding)</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>true</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(special-symbol? &#39;ns)</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>true</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(special-symbol? &#39;async)</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>true</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(special-symbol? &#39;new)</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(special-symbol? &#39;throw)</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(special-symbol? &#39;catch)</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(special-symbol? &#39;&amp;)</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>defmacro</code>, <code>binding</code> and <code>ns</code> are special forms here and vars on the JVM, which is also why <code>ns-publics</code> omits them.</p><h4 id="string-and-char-escapes-are-incomplete" tabindex="-1">String and char escapes are incomplete <a class="header-anchor" href="#string-and-char-escapes-are-incomplete" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;String and char escapes are incomplete&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>&quot;\\101&quot;</code> is <code>&quot;A&quot;</code> on the JVM and <code>&quot;101&quot;</code> here. <code>&quot;\\b\\f&quot;</code> is a backspace and a form feed there and <code>&quot;bf&quot;</code> here. The string scanner handles <code>\\&quot;</code>, <code>\\\\</code>, <code>\\n</code>, <code>\\r</code> and <code>\\t</code>, then keeps the next character for anything else, where the JVM reads every other unknown escape as an error.</p><p><code>\\o101</code> is <code>\\A</code> on the JVM and <code>Unknown character literal</code> here.</p><h4 id="strings-traverse-as-strings-not-chars" tabindex="-1">Strings traverse as strings, not chars <a class="header-anchor" href="#strings-traverse-as-strings-not-chars" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Strings traverse as strings, not chars&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(= \\a (first &quot;a&quot;))</code> is <code>true</code> on the JVM and <code>false</code> here, and <code>(seq &quot;ab&quot;)</code> gives <code>(&quot;a&quot; &quot;b&quot;)</code> instead of <code>(\\a \\b)</code>. <code>vec</code>, <code>set</code>, <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>reduce</code>, <code>frequencies</code>, <code>sort</code>, <code>zipmap</code> and <code>into</code> see the same strings, so <code>(filter #(= % \\a) &quot;aba&quot;)</code> is <code>()</code> here.</p><p>Indexing is missing too: <code>(get &quot;abc&quot; 1)</code> is <code>\\b</code> on the JVM and <code>nil</code> here, <code>(nth &quot;abc&quot; 1)</code> throws, and <code>(let [[a b] &quot;hi&quot;] [a b])</code> throws <code>Cannot destructure</code>. <code>(reverse &quot;ab&quot;)</code> is in <em>last, reverse and rest reject some seqables</em>.</p><p>cljam has a char type and <code>char?</code>, but <code>toSeq</code> maps a string&#39;s characters to strings. No host constraint forces this. It also walks code points where the JVM walks UTF-16 units, so <code>(seq &quot;😀&quot;)</code> has one element here and two there, while <code>(count &quot;😀&quot;)</code> is <code>2</code> on both. It stays open until traversal yields chars.</p><h4 id="thrown-values-are-not-exceptions" tabindex="-1">Thrown values are not exceptions <a class="header-anchor" href="#thrown-values-are-not-exceptions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Thrown values are not exceptions&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(throw 1)</code> is an error on the JVM (<code>Long</code> cannot be cast to <code>Throwable</code>). Here it throws <code>1</code>, and <code>(try (throw 1) (catch :default e e))</code> returns <code>1</code>.</p><p>A caught error is a map: <code>(map? e)</code> is <code>true</code> and <code>(:message e)</code> works, where the JVM gives <code>false</code> and <code>nil</code>. Host error text differs too: <code>(/ 1 0)</code> reports <code>division by zero</code> instead of <code>Divide by zero</code>, and <code>(ex-message e)</code> for <code>(nth [1] 5)</code> is a sentence where the JVM&#39;s is <code>nil</code>. <code>Throwable-&gt;map</code> is absent. Open until the error model is redesigned; see <em>catch does not test classes</em>.</p><h4 id="unresolved-symbols-fail-at-run-time" tabindex="-1">Unresolved symbols fail at run time <a class="header-anchor" href="#unresolved-symbols-fail-at-run-time" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Unresolved symbols fail at run time&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(fn [] undefined-sym)</code> and <code>(defn g [] (undefined-fn 1))</code> are compile errors on the JVM. Here both are accepted and fail only when called. <code>(if false undefined-sym 1)</code> is a compile error there and <code>1</code> here. A typo in a fn body stays hidden until that line runs.</p><h4 id="var-metadata-is-incomplete" tabindex="-1">Var metadata is incomplete <a class="header-anchor" href="#var-metadata-is-incomplete" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Var metadata is incomplete&quot;">​</a></h4><p>After <code>(defn f [] 1)</code>, <code>(:name (meta #&#39;f))</code> is <code>f</code> on the JVM and <code>nil</code> here, <code>(:ns (meta #&#39;f))</code> is the namespace and <code>nil</code> here, and <code>(:arglists (meta #&#39;f))</code> is <code>([])</code> and <code>[[]]</code> here. <code>(:macro (meta #&#39;when))</code> is <code>true</code> there and <code>nil</code> here, and core vars have no <code>:added</code>. Core docstrings are cljam&#39;s own text. <code>(ns foo &quot;doc&quot; {:a 1})</code> records no namespace metadata.</p><h3 id="loud-error-where-the-jvm-succeeds" tabindex="-1">Loud: error where the JVM succeeds <a class="header-anchor" href="#loud-error-where-the-jvm-succeeds" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Loud: error where the JVM succeeds&quot;">​</a></h3><h4 id="form-and-env-are-not-bound-in-macros" tabindex="-1">&amp;form and &amp;env are not bound in macros <a class="header-anchor" href="#form-and-env-are-not-bound-in-macros" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;&amp;form and &amp;env are not bound in macros&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(defmacro fm [] (list &#39;quote (some? &amp;form))) (fm)</code> is <code>true</code> on the JVM and <code>Symbol &amp;form not found</code> here. Macros that read <code>(:line (meta &amp;form))</code> or <code>(keys &amp;env)</code> fail the same way.</p><h4 id="case-rejects-lists-and-symbols-as-constants" tabindex="-1">case rejects lists and symbols as constants <a class="header-anchor" href="#case-rejects-lists-and-symbols-as-constants" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;case rejects lists and symbols as constants&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(case :a (:a :b) 1)</code> is <code>1</code> on the JVM and throws <code>No matching clause: :a</code> here. <code>(case &#39;a a 1)</code> is <code>1</code> there and <code>Symbol a not found</code> here. Keywords, numbers, strings and vectors work.</p><h4 id="compare-handles-few-types" tabindex="-1">compare handles few types <a class="header-anchor" href="#compare-handles-few-types" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;compare handles few types&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(compare [1 2] [1 3])</code> is <code>-1</code> on the JVM, <code>(compare &#39;a &#39;b)</code> is <code>-1</code> and <code>(compare true false)</code> is <code>1</code>. Here all three throw <code>compare: cannot compare</code>. <code>(sort [[3] [1 2]])</code>, <code>(sort [&#39;b &#39;a])</code> and <code>(sort [true false])</code> fail with them. Numbers, strings, keywords, chars and nil compare.</p><h4 id="conj-on-a-map-only-takes-pairs" tabindex="-1">conj on a map only takes pairs <a class="header-anchor" href="#conj-on-a-map-only-takes-pairs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;conj on a map only takes pairs&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(conj {:a 1} {:b 2})</code> is <code>{:a 1, :b 2}</code> on the JVM and throws here. <code>(conj {} {})</code> and <code>(conj {:a 1} nil)</code> throw too, and <code>(into {} [{:a 1} {:b 2}])</code> fails with them. <code>(merge {:a 1} [:b 2])</code> also throws. <code>(conj {:a 1} [:b 2])</code> works.</p><h4 id="defn-rejects-attribute-maps" tabindex="-1">defn rejects attribute maps <a class="header-anchor" href="#defn-rejects-attribute-maps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;defn rejects attribute maps&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(defn f {:a 1} [x] x)</code> and <code>(defn f &quot;d&quot; {:a 1} [x] x)</code> define <code>f</code> on the JVM, with <code>:a</code> on the var. Here both throw <code>first expects a collection or string</code>, though the <code>defn</code> docstring says it accepts an attribute map. <code>(defn f ([x] x) {:a 1})</code> throws <code>Unsupported binding form: :a</code>, and <code>(defmacro m {:a 1} [x] x)</code> throws <code>fn/defmacro expects a parameter vector or arity clauses</code>. Metadata on the name, <code>(defn ^:foo f [x] x)</code>, works.</p><h4 id="float-division-by-zero-throws" tabindex="-1">Float division by zero throws <a class="header-anchor" href="#float-division-by-zero-throws" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Float division by zero throws&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(/ 1.0 0.0)</code> is <code>##Inf</code> on the JVM, <code>(/ 0.0 0.0)</code> is <code>##NaN</code> and <code>(/ 1 0.0)</code> is <code>##Inf</code>. Here all three throw <code>division by zero</code>. Follows from <em>One double-precision number type</em>: <code>(/ 1 0)</code> must throw, and with one number type <code>1</code> and <code>1.0</code> look the same to <code>/</code>.</p><h4 id="keyword-and-symbol-accept-only-strings" tabindex="-1">keyword and symbol accept only strings <a class="header-anchor" href="#keyword-and-symbol-accept-only-strings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;keyword and symbol accept only strings&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(keyword &#39;a)</code> and <code>(keyword :a)</code> are <code>:a</code> on the JVM, <code>(keyword nil)</code> is <code>nil</code> and <code>(keyword 1)</code> is <code>nil</code>. <code>(symbol :a)</code> is <code>a</code> and <code>(symbol #&#39;map)</code> is <code>clojure.core/map</code>. Here each throws <code>expects a string</code>, except <code>(symbol &#39;a)</code>.</p><h4 id="last-reverse-and-rest-reject-some-seqables" tabindex="-1">last, reverse and rest reject some seqables <a class="header-anchor" href="#last-reverse-and-rest-reject-some-seqables" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;last, reverse and rest reject some seqables&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(reverse (range 3))</code> is <code>(2 1 0)</code> on the JVM and throws <code>reverse expects a list or vector</code> here. <code>(last (range 3))</code>, <code>(last (map inc [1]))</code>, <code>(reverse (map inc [1]))</code>, <code>(reverse (seq [1]))</code>, <code>(last #{1})</code> and <code>(reverse {:a 1})</code> all fail the same way. A vector or a realized list works.</p><p><code>(rest #{1 2})</code>, <code>(next #{1})</code> and <code>(second #{1})</code> throw <code>rest expects a collection or string</code>.</p><h4 id="many-functions-reject-nil" tabindex="-1">Many functions reject nil <a class="header-anchor" href="#many-functions-reject-nil" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Many functions reject nil&quot;">​</a></h4><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>JVM</th><th>cljam</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>(conj nil 1)</code></td><td><code>(1)</code></td><td>throws</td></tr><tr><td><code>(into nil [1])</code></td><td><code>(1)</code></td><td>throws</td></tr><tr><td><code>(dissoc nil :a)</code></td><td><code>nil</code></td><td>throws</td></tr><tr><td><code>(pop nil)</code></td><td><code>nil</code></td><td>throws</td></tr><tr><td><code>(keys nil)</code>, <code>(vals nil)</code></td><td><code>nil</code></td><td>throws</td></tr><tr><td><code>(nth nil 0)</code></td><td><code>nil</code></td><td>throws</td></tr><tr><td><code>(last nil)</code></td><td><code>nil</code></td><td>throws</td></tr><tr><td><code>(reverse nil)</code></td><td><code>()</code></td><td>throws</td></tr><tr><td><code>(zipmap nil nil)</code></td><td><code>{}</code></td><td>throws</td></tr><tr><td><code>(reduce-kv + 0 nil)</code></td><td><code>0</code></td><td>throws</td></tr><tr><td><code>(disj nil 1)</code></td><td><code>nil</code></td><td><code>#{}</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(seqable? nil)</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>first</code>, <code>rest</code>, <code>seq</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>assoc</code>, <code>merge</code> and <code>map</code> are nil-safe.</p><h4 id="most-numeric-literal-forms-do-not-read" tabindex="-1">Most numeric literal forms do not read <a class="header-anchor" href="#most-numeric-literal-forms-do-not-read" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Most numeric literal forms do not read&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>0xFF</code>, <code>2r101</code>, <code>36rZZ</code>, <code>1/2</code>, <code>1N</code> and <code>1.5M</code> read on the JVM. Here the number scanner stops at the first letter, so <code>0xFF</code> fails with <code>Symbol xFF not found</code> and <code>1/2</code> with <code>Symbol /2 not found</code>.</p><p><code>##Inf</code>, <code>##-Inf</code> and <code>##NaN</code> fail with <code>Unknown dispatch character: ##</code>, <code>1.</code> is <code>Invalid number format</code>, and <code>+1</code> is <code>Symbol +1 not found</code>. Which of these can exist follows from <em>One double-precision number type</em>.</p><h4 id="multimethod-dispatch-is-incomplete" tabindex="-1">Multimethod dispatch is incomplete <a class="header-anchor" href="#multimethod-dispatch-is-incomplete" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Multimethod dispatch is incomplete&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(defmulti hm identity :hierarchy (atom (derive (make-hierarchy) :a :b)))</code> dispatches through that hierarchy on the JVM. Here the option is ignored, so <code>(hm :a)</code> finds no method.</p><p>With <code>(derive ::sub ::mid) (derive ::mid ::top)</code> and methods on both <code>::mid</code> and <code>::top</code>, <code>(d ::sub)</code> picks <code>::mid</code> on the JVM and throws <code>Multiple methods</code> here.</p><p><code>(derive ::a ::b) (isa? [::a] [::b])</code> is <code>true</code> there and <code>false</code> here, so a method on <code>[::b ::b]</code> is not found for <code>[::a ::a]</code>. Deriving a tag from an ancestor it already has throws on the JVM and succeeds here.</p><h4 id="one-ns-form-per-file-or-evaluation" tabindex="-1">One ns form per file or evaluation <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-ns-form-per-file-or-evaluation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One ns form per file or evaluation&quot;">​</a></h4><p>A file with <code>(ns one) (def x 1) (ns two)</code> loads on the JVM. The CLI rejects it with <code>A file may declare at most one namespace</code>, and <code>session.evaluate</code> rejects the same source with <code>ns form must be the first form in an evaluation</code>. Each <code>ns</code> needs its own file or evaluation.</p><h4 id="print-and-str-crash-on-indexed-seqs" tabindex="-1">print and str crash on indexed seqs <a class="header-anchor" href="#print-and-str-crash-on-indexed-seqs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;print and str crash on indexed seqs&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(with-out-str (println (seq [1 2])))</code> is <code>&quot;(1 2)\\n&quot;</code> on the JVM and throws <code>unhandled value type: indexed-seq</code> here. So do <code>(str (seq [1 2]))</code>, <code>(print (next [1 2 3]))</code>, <code>(println (seq &quot;hi&quot;))</code>, <code>(println (seq {:a 1}))</code> and <code>(println (seq #{1}))</code>. <code>pr-str</code> and <code>prn</code> work.</p><p><code>valueToString</code> has no case for that value kind.</p><h4 id="protocols-cannot-extend-classes" tabindex="-1">Protocols cannot extend classes <a class="header-anchor" href="#protocols-cannot-extend-classes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Protocols cannot extend classes&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(extend-protocol P String (p [_] :s))</code> works on the JVM. Here it throws <code>expected a keyword type tag or nil, got: String</code>, and the same goes for <code>Object</code>, <code>Number</code>, <code>clojure.lang.Keyword</code> and a record name. The accepted form is <code>(extend-protocol P :string ...)</code>, with the tags that <code>type</code> returns.</p><p><code>(defrecord R [x] Object (toString [_] &quot;hi&quot;))</code> also fails, and <code>:extend-via-metadata</code> is not supported. <code>extend</code> and <code>extends?</code> are in <em>deftype, reify and extend are absent</em>. Open with <em>class, type and instance? have no class model</em>: what dispatch means depends on the class redesign.</p><h4 id="reduce-on-an-empty-coll-does-not-call-f" tabindex="-1">reduce on an empty coll does not call f <a class="header-anchor" href="#reduce-on-an-empty-coll-does-not-call-f" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;reduce on an empty coll does not call f&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(reduce + [])</code> is <code>0</code> on the JVM and <code>(reduce * [])</code> is <code>1</code>. Here both throw <code>reduce called on empty collection with no initial value</code>. So does <code>(reduce + nil)</code>, and the same with an empty list, map or lazy seq. <code>(apply + [])</code> works.</p><h4 id="symbols-cannot-be-called" tabindex="-1">Symbols cannot be called <a class="header-anchor" href="#symbols-cannot-be-called" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Symbols cannot be called&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(&#39;a {&#39;a 1})</code> is <code>1</code> on the JVM and <code>(&#39;a {} :d)</code> is <code>:d</code>. Here both throw <code>(quote a) is not callable</code>, and <code>(ifn? &#39;a)</code> is <code>false</code>. Keywords, maps, sets and vectors are callable.</p><h4 id="top-level-do-is-not-form-by-form" tabindex="-1">Top-level do is not form-by-form <a class="header-anchor" href="#top-level-do-is-not-form-by-form" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Top-level do is not form-by-form&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(do (defmacro m [] 1) (m))</code> is <code>1</code> on the JVM and <code>m is not callable</code> here. <code>(do (ns foo) *ns*)</code> is <code>foo</code> there and <code>user</code> here.</p><h4 id="trailing-map-in-kwargs-calls-fails" tabindex="-1">Trailing map in kwargs calls fails <a class="header-anchor" href="#trailing-map-in-kwargs-calls-fails" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Trailing map in kwargs calls fails&quot;">​</a></h4><p>With <code>(defn kw [&amp; {:keys [a]}] a)</code>, <code>(kw {:a 1})</code> is <code>1</code> on the JVM and throws <code>hash-map expects an even number of arguments</code> here. <code>(kw :a 1)</code> works. A mix also fails: with <code>(defn kw [&amp; {:as m}] m)</code>, <code>(kw :a 1 {:b 2})</code> is <code>{:a 1, :b 2}</code> on the JVM.</p><h4 id="vars-are-not-values-you-can-deref-or-resolve-to" tabindex="-1">Vars are not values you can deref or resolve to <a class="header-anchor" href="#vars-are-not-values-you-can-deref-or-resolve-to" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Vars are not values you can deref or resolve to&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(def v 1) @#&#39;v</code> is <code>1</code> on the JVM and fails here with <code>deref expects an atom, volatile, reduced, or delay value, got var</code>. <code>(deref (var v))</code> fails the same way; <code>var-get</code> works.</p><p><code>resolve</code> returns the value, so <code>(var? (resolve &#39;v))</code> is <code>false</code> and <code>(resolve &#39;clojure.core/map)</code> is the function.</p><p><code>(def x) x</code> is an unbound-var object on the JVM and <code>Symbol x not found</code> here, and <code>(declare x) #&#39;x</code> fails with <code>Unable to resolve var</code>. A var declared this way works once it is defined.</p><h3 id="output-and-printing" tabindex="-1">Output and printing <a class="header-anchor" href="#output-and-printing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Output and printing&quot;">​</a></h3><h4 id="maps-print-without-commas" tabindex="-1">Maps print without commas <a class="header-anchor" href="#maps-print-without-commas" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Maps print without commas&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(pr-str {:a 1 :b 2})</code> is <code>&quot;{:a 1, :b 2}&quot;</code> on the JVM and <code>&quot;{:a 1 :b 2}&quot;</code> here. <code>str</code> and <code>print</code> do the same, and so do namespaced maps: <code>#:a{:b 1, :c 2}</code> against <code>#:a{:b 1 :c 2}</code>. Both read back as equal values.</p><h4 id="nan-and-infinities-print-in-javascript-form" tabindex="-1">NaN and infinities print in JavaScript form <a class="header-anchor" href="#nan-and-infinities-print-in-javascript-form" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;NaN and infinities print in JavaScript form&quot;">​</a></h4><p>With <code>clojure.math</code> as <code>m</code>, <code>(pr-str (m/sqrt -1))</code> is <code>&quot;##NaN&quot;</code> on the JVM and <code>&quot;NaN&quot;</code> here. <code>(pr-str (m/pow 10 400))</code> is <code>&quot;##Inf&quot;</code> and <code>&quot;Infinity&quot;</code>, and the negative case is <code>&quot;##-Inf&quot;</code> and <code>&quot;-Infinity&quot;</code>. <code>str</code> is <code>&quot;NaN&quot;</code> on both.</p><h4 id="opaque-values-print-in-cljam-formats" tabindex="-1">Opaque values print in cljam formats <a class="header-anchor" href="#opaque-values-print-in-cljam-formats" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Opaque values print in cljam formats&quot;">​</a></h4><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>JVM</th><th>cljam</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>(pr-str (atom 1))</code></td><td><code>#object[clojure.lang.Atom 0x... {:status :ready, :val 1}]</code></td><td><code>#&lt;Atom 1&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(pr-str (delay 1))</code></td><td><code>#object[clojure.lang.Delay 0x... {...}]</code></td><td><code>#&lt;Delay pending&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(pr-str inc)</code></td><td><code>#object[clojure.core$inc 0x... &quot;...&quot;]</code></td><td><code>(native-fn inc)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(pr-str (fn []))</code></td><td><code>#object[...$fn__N 0x... &quot;...&quot;]</code></td><td><code>#function[user/eval174/fn--779]</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(pr-str *ns*)</code></td><td><code>#object[clojure.lang.Namespace 0x... &quot;user&quot;]</code></td><td><code>#namespace[user]</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>(pr-str (ex-info &quot;x&quot; {}))</code></td><td><code>#error {...}</code></td><td><code>{:message &quot;x&quot; :data {}}</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The JVM forms name JVM classes and identity hashes, so what they become depends on <em>class, type and instance? have no class model</em>.</p><h4 id="records-print-with-a-slash-tag" tabindex="-1">Records print with a slash tag <a class="header-anchor" href="#records-print-with-a-slash-tag" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Records print with a slash tag&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(pr-str (-&gt;P 1))</code> is <code>&quot;#user.P{:x 1}&quot;</code> on the JVM and <code>&quot;#user/P{:x 1}&quot;</code> here. On the JVM <code>read-string</code> returns the record. Here it throws <code>Reader tags (#user/P) are only supported in EDN mode</code>. <code>(str (-&gt;P 1))</code> also prints the fields, where the JVM prints <code>user.P@hash</code>.</p><h4 id="some-reader-errors-report-0-based-positions" tabindex="-1">Some reader errors report 0-based positions <a class="header-anchor" href="#some-reader-errors-report-0-based-positions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Some reader errors report 0-based positions&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>\\o101</code> on the first line reports <code>at line 0 column 0</code>, <code>1.</code> the same, and an unterminated string reports a raw offset. Parser errors add 1, so <code>(read-string &quot;(&quot;)</code> says <code>line 1 column 1</code>.</p><h4 id="str-on-a-collection-drops-string-quotes" tabindex="-1">str on a collection drops string quotes <a class="header-anchor" href="#str-on-a-collection-drops-string-quotes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;str on a collection drops string quotes&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(str [&quot;a&quot;])</code> is <code>&quot;[\\&quot;a\\&quot;]&quot;</code> on the JVM and <code>&quot;[a]&quot;</code> here. <code>(str {:a &quot;b&quot;})</code>, <code>(str &#39;(&quot;a&quot;))</code> and <code>(str #{&quot;a&quot;})</code> behave the same.</p><p><code>(str (map inc [1]))</code> is <code>&quot;clojure.lang.LazySeq@21&quot;</code> on the JVM and <code>&quot;(2)&quot;</code> here, and <code>(str (fn []))</code> is a class name there and <code>&quot;(fn [] )&quot;</code> here.</p><h4 id="the-node-output-sink-adds-a-newline-per-write" tabindex="-1">The Node output sink adds a newline per write <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-node-output-sink-adds-a-newline-per-write" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Node output sink adds a newline per write&quot;">​</a></h4><p>A file holding <code>(print &quot;a&quot;) (print &quot;b&quot;) (println &quot;c&quot;)</code> writes <code>abc</code> and one newline on the JVM. The CLI writes <code>a</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>c</code> on separate lines with a blank line after, and <code>pr</code> and <code>prn</code> do the same. The sink appends a newline to text that already carries its own.</p><p><code>with-out-str</code> is unaffected and matches the JVM byte for byte.</p><h4 id="time-prints-without-quotes" tabindex="-1">time prints without quotes <a class="header-anchor" href="#time-prints-without-quotes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;time prints without quotes&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(time 1)</code> prints <code>&quot;Elapsed time: 0.003 msecs&quot;</code> with quotes on the JVM and <code>Elapsed time: 0.196 msecs</code> without them here.</p><h4 id="uncaught-ex-info-prints-object-object" tabindex="-1">Uncaught ex-info prints [object Object] <a class="header-anchor" href="#uncaught-ex-info-prints-object-object" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Uncaught ex-info prints [object Object]&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(throw (ex-info &quot;boom&quot; {:a 1}))</code> in a file run by the CLI writes <code>[object Object]</code>. The JVM reports <code>Execution error (ExceptionInfo) at user/eval (a.clj:2).</code>, the message, and a full report. The handler stringifies a thrown value that is not an <code>Error</code>.</p><h3 id="not-implemented" tabindex="-1">Not implemented <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-implemented" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not implemented&quot;">​</a></h3><h4 id="inst-and-uuid-do-not-read-in-code" tabindex="-1">#inst and #uuid do not read in code <a class="header-anchor" href="#inst-and-uuid-do-not-read-in-code" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;#inst and #uuid do not read in code&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>#inst &quot;2020-01-01&quot;</code> and <code>#uuid &quot;...&quot;</code> evaluate on the JVM. Here they fail with <code>Reader tags (#inst) are only supported in EDN mode</code>, as does <code>(read-string &quot;#inst \\&quot;2020-01-01\\&quot;&quot;)</code>. <code>clojure.edn/read-string</code> accepts them, but the result prints without its tag: <code>(pr-str (edn/read-string &quot;#inst \\&quot;2020-01-01\\&quot;&quot;))</code> is <code>2020-01-01T00:00:00.000Z</code>, not <code>#inst &quot;...&quot;</code>.</p><p>Absent vars: <code>tagged-literal</code>, <code>tagged-literal?</code>, <code>default-data-readers</code>, <code>*default-data-reader-fn*</code>, <code>random-uuid</code>, <code>parse-uuid</code>, <code>uuid?</code>, <code>inst?</code>, <code>inst-ms</code>, <code>inst-ms*</code>, <code>Inst</code>. <code>#=</code> is <code>Unknown dispatch character</code>.</p><h4 id="concurrency-primitives-are-absent" tabindex="-1">Concurrency primitives are absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#concurrency-primitives-are-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Concurrency primitives are absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>@(future 1)</code>, <code>(ref 1)</code> and <code>(agent 1)</code> are <code>Symbol not found</code>. Deref cannot block outside <em>async is a lexical boundary</em>, so <code>future</code> needs a decision, while <code>promise</code> and <code>ref</code> look direct to port.</p><p>Absent vars: <code>future</code>, <code>future-call</code>, <code>future-cancel</code>, <code>future-cancelled?</code>, <code>future-done?</code>, <code>future?</code>, <code>promise</code>, <code>deliver</code>, <code>ref</code>, <code>ref-set</code>, <code>ref-history-count</code>, <code>ref-max-history</code>, <code>ref-min-history</code>, <code>alter</code>, <code>commute</code>, <code>ensure</code>, <code>dosync</code>, <code>sync</code>, <code>io!</code>, <code>locking</code>, <code>agent</code>, <code>*agent*</code>, <code>agent-error</code>, <code>agent-errors</code>, <code>clear-agent-errors</code>, <code>restart-agent</code>, <code>error-handler</code>, <code>error-mode</code>, <code>set-error-handler!</code>, <code>set-error-mode!</code>, <code>send</code>, <code>send-off</code>, <code>send-via</code>, <code>set-agent-send-executor!</code>, <code>set-agent-send-off-executor!</code>, <code>release-pending-sends</code>, <code>shutdown-agents</code>, <code>await</code>, <code>await-for</code>, <code>await1</code>, <code>pmap</code>, <code>pcalls</code>, <code>pvalues</code>.</p><h4 id="core-dynamic-vars-are-absent" tabindex="-1">Core dynamic vars are absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#core-dynamic-vars-are-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Core dynamic vars are absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(binding [*print-meta* true] ...)</code> fails with <code>No var found for symbol</code>, and so does <code>*print-readably*</code>. <code>*out*</code> and <code>*err*</code> exist but are <code>nil</code>, and <code>(clojure-version)</code> is <code>&quot;1.12.0&quot;</code>.</p><p>Absent vars: <code>*1</code>, <code>*2</code>, <code>*3</code>, <code>*e</code>, <code>*assert*</code>, <code>*clojure-version*</code>, <code>*command-line-args*</code>, <code>*file*</code>, <code>*flush-on-newline*</code>, <code>*in*</code>, <code>*math-context*</code>, <code>*print-dup*</code>, <code>*print-meta*</code>, <code>*print-namespace-maps*</code>, <code>*print-readably*</code>, <code>*read-eval*</code>, <code>*repl*</code>, <code>*unchecked-math*</code>.</p><h4 id="deftype-reify-and-extend-are-absent" tabindex="-1">deftype, reify and extend are absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#deftype-reify-and-extend-are-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;deftype, reify and extend are absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(deftype T [x])</code>, <code>(reify P (f [_] :ok))</code>, <code>(extend String P {...})</code> and <code>(extends? P String)</code> are <code>Symbol not found</code>. Absent vars: <code>deftype</code>, <code>reify</code>, <code>extend</code>, <code>extends?</code>. <code>defprotocol</code>, <code>defrecord</code>, <code>extend-type</code> and <code>extend-protocol</code> exist; see <em>Protocols cannot extend classes</em>.</p><h4 id="everyday-collection-functions-are-absent" tabindex="-1">Everyday collection functions are absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#everyday-collection-functions-are-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Everyday collection functions are absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Absent: <code>ffirst</code>, <code>tree-seq</code>, <code>replace</code>, <code>replicate</code>, <code>distinct?</code>, <code>bounded-count</code>, <code>comparator</code>, <code>array-map</code>, <code>map-entry?</code>, <code>indexed?</code>, <code>partitionv</code>, <code>partitionv-all</code>, <code>splitv-at</code>, <code>vector-of</code>, <code>find-keyword</code>, <code>xml-seq</code>.</p><h4 id="everyday-macros-are-absent" tabindex="-1">Everyday macros are absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#everyday-macros-are-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Everyday macros are absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Absent: <code>if-not</code>, <code>doto</code>, <code>defonce</code>, <code>assert</code>, <code>lazy-cat</code>, <code>with-open</code>, <code>definline</code>. <code>doto</code> and <code>with-open</code> would need host calls, the others are plain Clojure. <code>(if-not false 1)</code> fails with <code>Symbol if-not not found</code>.</p><h4 id="file-and-stream-io-is-absent" tabindex="-1">File and stream IO is absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#file-and-stream-io-is-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;File and stream IO is absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(slurp &quot;/etc/hosts&quot;)</code> and <code>(spit &quot;x&quot; &quot;a&quot;)</code> are <code>Symbol not found</code>, also under <code>nodePreset</code>. A browser host has no file system, so these need a host capability, not a browser polyfill.</p><p>Absent vars: <code>slurp</code>, <code>spit</code>, <code>read</code>, <code>read+string</code>, <code>read-line</code>, <code>line-seq</code>, <code>file-seq</code>, <code>with-in-str</code>, <code>*in*</code>.</p><h4 id="format-and-printing-extension-are-absent" tabindex="-1">format and printing extension are absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#format-and-printing-extension-are-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;format and printing extension are absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(format &quot;%d %s&quot; 1 &quot;a&quot;)</code> is <code>&quot;1 a&quot;</code> on the JVM and <code>Symbol format not found</code> here. On the JVM <code>format</code> delegates to <code>java.util.Formatter</code>, so its directives need a host-side definition.</p><p>Absent vars: <code>format</code>, <code>printf</code>, <code>flush</code>, <code>print-method</code>, <code>print-dup</code>, <code>print-simple</code>, <code>print-ctor</code>, <code>char-escape-string</code>, <code>char-name-string</code>.</p><h4 id="host-arrays-are-absent" tabindex="-1">Host arrays are absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#host-arrays-are-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Host arrays are absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Absent vars: <code>aclone</code>, <code>aget</code>, <code>alength</code>, <code>amap</code>, <code>areduce</code>, <code>aset</code>, <code>aset-boolean</code>, <code>aset-byte</code>, <code>aset-char</code>, <code>aset-double</code>, <code>aset-float</code>, <code>aset-int</code>, <code>aset-long</code>, <code>aset-short</code>, <code>make-array</code>, <code>into-array</code>, <code>to-array</code>, <code>to-array-2d</code>, <code>object-array</code>, <code>boolean-array</code>, <code>byte-array</code>, <code>char-array</code>, <code>double-array</code>, <code>float-array</code>, <code>int-array</code>, <code>long-array</code>, <code>short-array</code>, <code>booleans</code>, <code>bytes</code>, <code>bytes?</code>, <code>chars</code>, <code>doubles</code>, <code>floats</code>, <code>ints</code>, <code>longs</code>, <code>shorts</code>.</p><h4 id="missing-arities" tabindex="-1">Missing arities <a class="header-anchor" href="#missing-arities" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Missing arities&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(doall 2 (range 5))</code> and <code>(dorun 2 (range 5))</code> throw <code>No matching arity</code>. The zero-argument <code>distinct</code> is in <em>Transducer gaps</em>, and the options arity of <code>read-string</code> is in <em>read-string takes no options and accepts empty input</em>.</p><h4 id="multimethod-api-is-absent" tabindex="-1">Multimethod API is absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#multimethod-api-is-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Multimethod API is absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>prefer-method</code>, <code>prefers</code>, <code>methods</code>, <code>get-method</code>, <code>remove-method</code> and <code>remove-all-methods</code> are <code>Symbol not found</code>, so an ambiguous dispatch cannot be resolved with a preference.</p><h4 id="namespace-tooling-is-partial" tabindex="-1">Namespace tooling is partial <a class="header-anchor" href="#namespace-tooling-is-partial" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Namespace tooling is partial&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(require &#39;clojure.string)</code>, <code>(require &#39;(clojure string))</code> and <code>(ns foo (:require clojure.string))</code> work on the JVM. Here they throw <code>require spec must be a vector</code>; only <code>(require &#39;[clojure.string])</code> works. <code>:refer :all</code> and <code>:rename</code> are rejected too, and <code>(ns foo &quot;doc&quot; {:a 1})</code> drops its metadata.</p><p>Absent vars: <code>alias</code>, <code>ns-resolve</code>, <code>requiring-resolve</code>, <code>use</code>, <code>refer</code>, <code>refer-clojure</code>, <code>create-ns</code>, <code>remove-ns</code>, <code>ns-unmap</code>, <code>ns-unalias</code>, <code>find-var</code>, <code>intern</code>, <code>load</code>, <code>load-file</code>, <code>load-reader</code>, <code>load-string</code>, <code>munge</code>, <code>namespace-munge</code>. <code>ns-refers</code> returns <code>{}</code>, and <code>(str *ns*)</code> is <code>&quot;#namespace[user]&quot;</code>, not <code>&quot;user&quot;</code>.</p><h4 id="numeric-tower-api-is-absent" tabindex="-1">Numeric tower API is absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#numeric-tower-api-is-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Numeric tower API is absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(== 1 1.0)</code>, <code>(double 1)</code> and <code>(long 1.9)</code> are <code>Symbol not found</code> here, while <code>int</code> exists.</p><p>Absent vars: <code>==</code>, <code>*&#39;</code>, <code>+&#39;</code>, <code>-&#39;</code>, <code>inc&#39;</code>, <code>dec&#39;</code>, <code>num</code>, <code>double</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>long</code>, <code>short</code>, <code>byte</code>, <code>bigdec</code>, <code>bigint</code>, <code>biginteger</code>, <code>decimal?</code>, <code>ratio?</code>, <code>rational?</code>, <code>rationalize</code>, <code>numerator</code>, <code>denominator</code>, <code>with-precision</code>, <code>bit-and-not</code>, <code>bit-clear</code>, <code>bit-flip</code>, <code>bit-set</code>, <code>bit-test</code>, and the <code>unchecked-*</code> family. What each means follows from <em>One double-precision number type</em>.</p><h4 id="reader-conditionals-are-not-implemented" tabindex="-1">Reader conditionals are not implemented <a class="header-anchor" href="#reader-conditionals-are-not-implemented" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reader conditionals are not implemented&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>#?(:default 3)</code> is <code>3</code> on the JVM and <code>Unknown dispatch character: #?</code> here. <code>[#?@(:default [1 2])]</code> fails the same way. The JVM also takes <code>(read-string {:read-cond :allow} &quot;#?(:clj 1)&quot;)</code>, covered by <em>read-string takes no options and accepts empty input</em>.</p><p>Implementing it follows <em>reader-features</em>. <code>reader-conditional</code> and <code>reader-conditional?</code> are absent.</p><h4 id="regex-matcher-api-is-absent" tabindex="-1">Regex matcher API is absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#regex-matcher-api-is-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Regex matcher API is absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(let [m (re-matcher #&quot;a&quot; &quot;a&quot;)] (re-find m) (re-groups m))</code> is <code>&quot;a&quot;</code> on the JVM and <code>Symbol re-matcher not found</code> here. <code>re-find</code>, <code>re-matches</code> and <code>re-seq</code> work on strings.</p><h4 id="sorted-collections-are-absent" tabindex="-1">Sorted collections are absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#sorted-collections-are-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sorted collections are absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Absent vars: <code>sorted-map</code>, <code>sorted-map-by</code>, <code>sorted-set</code>, <code>sorted-set-by</code>, <code>sorted?</code>, <code>subseq</code>, <code>rsubseq</code>, <code>rseq</code>, <code>reversible?</code>. <code>rseq</code> is absent for vectors too: <code>(rseq [1 2 3])</code> is <code>(3 2 1)</code> on the JVM.</p><h4 id="standard-namespaces-are-absent" tabindex="-1">Standard namespaces are absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#standard-namespaces-are-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Standard namespaces are absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(require &#39;[clojure.zip :as z])</code> fails with <code>Namespace &#39;clojure.zip&#39; not found</code>. The same for <code>clojure.pprint</code>, <code>clojure.repl</code>, <code>clojure.data</code>, <code>clojure.template</code>, <code>clojure.stacktrace</code>, <code>clojure.java.io</code>, <code>clojure.java.shell</code>, <code>clojure.spec.alpha</code>, <code>clojure.instant</code>, <code>clojure.xml</code>, <code>clojure.main</code>, <code>clojure.core.protocols</code>, <code>clojure.core.reducers</code>, <code>clojure.datafy</code>, <code>clojure.uuid</code> and <code>clojure.inspector</code>.</p><p><code>pprint</code> and <code>doc</code> exist in <code>clojure.core</code> instead; see <em>clojure.core carries non-Clojure names</em>.</p><h4 id="standard-namespaces-are-partial" tabindex="-1">Standard namespaces are partial <a class="header-anchor" href="#standard-namespaces-are-partial" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Standard namespaces are partial&quot;">​</a></h4><ul><li><code>clojure.math</code>, 17 of 45 absent: <code>IEEE-remainder</code>, <code>add-exact</code>, <code>copy-sign</code>, <code>decrement-exact</code>, <code>expm1</code>, <code>get-exponent</code>, <code>increment-exact</code>, <code>log1p</code>, <code>multiply-exact</code>, <code>negate-exact</code>, <code>next-after</code>, <code>next-down</code>, <code>next-up</code>, <code>random</code>, <code>scalb</code>, <code>subtract-exact</code>, <code>ulp</code>.</li><li><code>clojure.test</code>, 24 of 39 absent, including <code>test-ns</code>, <code>test-var</code>, <code>test-vars</code>, <code>run-all-tests</code>, <code>run-test-var</code>, <code>with-test</code>, <code>try-expr</code>, <code>do-report</code>, <code>assert-expr</code> and <code>deftest-</code>. <code>run-tests</code> returns no <code>:type :summary</code>.</li><li><code>clojure.walk</code> lacks <code>macroexpand-all</code>, <code>postwalk-demo</code> and <code>prewalk-demo</code>. <code>clojure.edn</code> lacks <code>read</code>.</li></ul><p><code>clojure.string</code> and <code>clojure.set</code> are complete.</p><h4 id="struct-maps-are-absent" tabindex="-1">Struct maps are absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#struct-maps-are-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Struct maps are absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Absent vars: <code>defstruct</code>, <code>create-struct</code>, <code>struct</code>, <code>struct-map</code>, <code>accessor</code>.</p><h4 id="tap-is-absent" tabindex="-1">tap&gt; is absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#tap-is-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;tap&gt; is absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Absent vars: <code>tap&gt;</code>, <code>add-tap</code>, <code>remove-tap</code>.</p><h4 id="transducer-gaps" tabindex="-1">Transducer gaps <a class="header-anchor" href="#transducer-gaps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Transducer gaps&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(into [] cat [[1] [2]])</code> is <code>[1 2]</code> on the JVM and <code>Symbol cat not found</code> here. <code>(into [] (distinct) [1 1 2])</code> fails because <code>distinct</code> has no transducer arity.</p><p>Absent vars: <code>cat</code>, <code>eduction</code>, <code>halt-when</code>, <code>random-sample</code>, <code>iteration</code>, <code>seque</code>, <code>stream-into!</code>, <code>stream-reduce!</code>, <code>stream-seq!</code>, <code>stream-transduce!</code>.</p><h4 id="transients-are-absent" tabindex="-1">Transients are absent <a class="header-anchor" href="#transients-are-absent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Transients are absent&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Absent vars: <code>transient</code>, <code>persistent!</code>, <code>conj!</code>, <code>assoc!</code>, <code>dissoc!</code>, <code>pop!</code>, <code>disj!</code>. Code written for speed with <code>(persistent! (reduce conj! (transient []) xs))</code> fails with <code>Symbol transient not found</code>.</p><h4 id="var-and-binding-api-is-partial" tabindex="-1">Var and binding API is partial <a class="header-anchor" href="#var-and-binding-api-is-partial" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Var and binding API is partial&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(bound? #&#39;v)</code> and <code>(thread-bound? #&#39;v)</code> work on the JVM and are <code>Symbol not found</code> here.</p><p>Absent vars: <code>bound?</code>, <code>thread-bound?</code>, <code>bound-fn</code>, <code>bound-fn*</code>, <code>var-set</code>, <code>with-local-vars</code>, <code>with-bindings</code>, <code>with-bindings*</code>, <code>with-redefs-fn</code>, <code>get-thread-bindings</code>, <code>push-thread-bindings</code>, <code>pop-thread-bindings</code>, <code>test</code>.</p><h3 id="justified-differences-with-a-stated-reason" tabindex="-1">Justified: differences with a stated reason <a class="header-anchor" href="#justified-differences-with-a-stated-reason" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Justified: differences with a stated reason&quot;">​</a></h3><h4 id="async-is-a-lexical-boundary" tabindex="-1">async is a lexical boundary <a class="header-anchor" href="#async-is-a-lexical-boundary" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;async is a lexical boundary&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(async 1)</code> has no JVM counterpart. <code>@(async 1)</code> outside <code>async</code> throws <code>@ on a pending value requires an (async ...) context</code>, where a JVM deref of a pending value blocks. A nested fn does not inherit the context: <code>(async (let [f (fn [] @(async 1))] (f)))</code> rejects with the same error.</p><p><strong>Reason.</strong> JavaScript has one thread and cannot block on a pending value, so <code>@</code> can only suspend inside an <code>async</code> body. As in JavaScript, a closure is not <code>async</code> because the code around it is: it needs its own <code>(async ...)</code>.</p><h4 id="no-java-interop" tabindex="-1">No Java interop <a class="header-anchor" href="#no-java-interop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;No Java interop&quot;">​</a></h4><p><code>(.toUpperCase &quot;a&quot;)</code>, <code>(String. &quot;a&quot;)</code>, <code>(new String &quot;a&quot;)</code>, <code>Long/MAX_VALUE</code>, <code>(Math/floor 1.5)</code>, <code>(System/currentTimeMillis)</code> and <code>(import java.util.Date)</code> fail here. In <code>(ns foo (:import java.util.Date))</code> the import has no effect.</p><p><strong>Reason.</strong> The host is JavaScript, so Java classes do not exist. Host access goes through the <code>js</code> module.</p><p>Absent because they describe JVM classes, bytecode or compilation: <code>..</code>, <code>memfn</code>, <code>import</code>, <code>bean</code>, <code>bases</code>, <code>supers</code>, <code>cast</code>, <code>compile</code>, <code>definterface</code>, <code>gen-class</code>, <code>gen-interface</code>, <code>proxy</code>, <code>construct-proxy</code>, <code>get-proxy-class</code>, <code>init-proxy</code>, <code>update-proxy</code>, <code>proxy-call-with-super</code>, <code>proxy-mappings</code>, <code>proxy-name</code>, <code>proxy-super</code>, <code>add-classpath</code>, <code>enumeration-seq</code>, <code>iterator-seq</code>, <code>resultset-seq</code>, <code>PrintWriter-on</code>, <code>StackTraceElement-&gt;vec</code>, <code>Throwable-&gt;map</code>, <code>method-sig</code>, <code>uri?</code>, <code>primitives-classnames</code>, <code>with-loading-context</code>, <code>-&gt;ArrayChunk</code>, <code>-&gt;Eduction</code>, <code>-&gt;Vec</code>, <code>-&gt;VecNode</code>, <code>-&gt;VecSeq</code>, <code>EMPTY-NODE</code>, <code>-cache-protocol-fn</code>, <code>-reset-methods</code>, <code>find-protocol-impl</code>, <code>find-protocol-method</code>, <code>seq-to-map-for-destructuring</code>, <code>*allow-unresolved-vars*</code>, <code>*compile-files*</code>, <code>*compile-path*</code>, <code>*fn-loader*</code>, <code>*reader-resolver*</code>, <code>*source-path*</code>, <code>*suppress-read*</code>, <code>*use-context-classloader*</code>, <code>*verbose-defrecords*</code>, <code>*warn-on-reflection*</code>.</p><h4 id="recursion-has-a-fixed-frame-budget" tabindex="-1">Recursion has a fixed frame budget <a class="header-anchor" href="#recursion-has-a-fixed-frame-budget" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Recursion has a fixed frame budget&quot;">​</a></h4><p>With <code>(defn f [n] (if (= n 0) 0 (inc (f (dec n)))))</code>, <code>(f 50000)</code> overflows a default JVM stack and returns <code>50000</code> here. <code>(f 150000)</code> overflows both, here as <code>Stack overflow: exceeded 100000 VM call frames</code>. Both are catchable.</p><p>Self tail calls reuse the frame, so <code>(defn f [n] (if (= n 0) :done (f (dec n))))</code> finishes for any <code>n</code> and a self tail call with no base case runs forever, where the JVM overflows.</p><p><strong>Reason.</strong> The JavaScript call stack is small and differs per engine. A VM budget gives one limit on every host, deeper than the JVM default.</p>`,259)])])}const m=o(a,[["render",r]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
